I deleted an entire API layer this week. A form now looks like this:

```tsx
export default function ReplyForm() {
  return (
    <form action={createReply}>
      <Editor name="body" />
      <button type="submit">Reply</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

No fetch calls, no loading flags I forgot to reset. Anyone else enjoying this?

Every few weeks someone posts a layout where the main column pushes past the edge of the screen, and nine times out of ten the cause is the same. It deserves its own thread, so here it is.

## The symptom

You have a sidebar and a content column. It looks perfect until the content contains something that refuses to shrink: a long code block, a wide table, an unbroken URL. Then the whole page gets a horizontal scrollbar and the sidebar is squeezed or shoved off screen.

## The cause

`1fr` does not mean "one share of the available space". It is shorthand for `minmax(auto, 1fr)`, and that `auto` minimum resolves to the min-content size of whatever is inside the track. A `<pre>` with one 900 pixel line has a min-content width of 900 pixels, so the track is not allowed to be narrower than that.

> A grid track will not shrink below the minimum size of its content unless you tell it that it may.

## The fix

Give the track an explicit minimum of zero:

```css
.layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 240px minmax(0, 1fr);
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

.layout pre {
  overflow-x: auto;
}
```

Now the column is allowed to shrink, and the code block scrolls inside itself instead of stretching the page.

## Things worth knowing

- `min-width: 0` on the grid item does the same job, and is the version you need for flex items, where the same rule applies.
- `overflow: hidden` on the item also works, but it clips focus outlines and shadows, so I avoid it.
- `repeat(3, 1fr)` has the same problem. Write `repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr))` when you want three columns that are truly equal.
- Long words and URLs additionally need `overflow-wrap: anywhere` to break.

If you have a layout that still overflows after this, post the smallest version of it below and we will work through it together.

My tailoring site has a contact page with only a few lines on it. On that page the footer sits right under the text, halfway up the screen, with a big empty space below it. On longer pages it looks fine.

I tried `position: fixed` on the footer, but then it covers the content on the long pages. This is my current CSS:

```css
body {
  margin: 0;
}

footer {
  padding: 2rem;
  background: #1f2a37;
  color: white;
}
```

The HTML is just `header`, `main` and `footer` inside `body`. What am I missing?

You are missing very little. The body is only as tall as its content, so on a short page it ends halfway up the screen and the footer ends with it. Make the body at least as tall as the viewport and let `main` take the spare room:

```css
body {
  margin: 0;
  min-height: 100dvh;
  display: grid;
  grid-template-rows: auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
}
```

Three rows: the header takes what it needs, `main` takes everything left over, and the footer takes what it needs. On long pages nothing changes, because `min-height` is only a minimum.
